"# What are the key properties of an RDD in Apache Spark?\n\nAn RDD (Resilient Distributed Dataset) is an immutable, distributed, fault-tolerant collection of data that supports parallel processing, lazy evaluation, and optional in-memory caching.\n\n## 1. Key Properties of RDD\n\n### 1. Immutability\n\nOnce an RDD is created, its contents cannot be modified in place using RDD transformations.\n\nEvery transformation creates a new RDD rather than modifying the existing one.\n\n```\nrdd1 = sc.parallelize([1, 2, 3, 4])\nrdd2 = rdd1.map(lambda x: x * 2)\n\nprint(rdd1.collect())  # [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(rdd2.collect())  # [2, 4, 6, 8]\n```\n\nBenefit: Immutability simplifies distributed processing, dependency tracking, and failure recovery.\n\n### 2. Distributed and Partitioned\n\nAn RDD is logically divided into multiple partitions that can be processed across different Executors.\n\n- Each partition contains a subset of the dataset.\n- Spark typically creates one task per partition within a stage.\n- Multiple partitions can be processed simultaneously.\n- The number of partitions influences available parallelism.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nrdd = sc.parallelize(\n    [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60],\n    3\n)\n\nprint(rdd.getNumPartitions())  # 3\n```\n\n[spark-reference:rdd-partitions]\n\nThree partitions can be processed concurrently if sufficient task slots are available.\n\n### 3. Fault Tolerance (Resilience)\n\nRDDs are fault-tolerant because Spark maintains lineage information, which records how an RDD was derived from other RDDs.\n\nIf an Executor fails and a partition is lost, Spark can recompute the lost partition using its dependencies, assuming the required source data remains accessible.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nRDD1 [Original Data]\n        |\n      filter()\n        |\nRDD2 [Filtered Data]\n        |\n       map()\n        |\nRDD3 [Final Data]\n```\n\nIf a partition of RDD3 is lost, Spark can reconstruct it by rerunning the necessary transformations.\n\nBenefit: Spark does not need to replicate every computed RDD partition to provide fault tolerance.\n\n### 4. Lazy Evaluation\n\nRDD transformations are not immediately executed.\n\nSpark records the transformations and builds a dependency graph. Computation begins when an action is executed.\n\n```\nrdd = sc.parallelize([1, 2, 3, 4])\nrdd2 = rdd.filter(lambda x: x > 2)  # Lazy\nrdd3 = rdd2.map(lambda x: x * 10)   # Lazy\n\nprint(rdd3.collect())               # Action\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n[30, 40]\n```\n\nBenefit: Lazy evaluation enables Spark to schedule transformations efficiently and pipeline compatible operations.\n\n### 5. In-Memory Persistence\n\nRDDs support caching and persistence, allowing computed partitions to be reused rather than recalculated.\n\n```\nrdd = sc.parallelize(range(1000000))\nrdd2 = rdd.map(lambda x: x * 2)\nrdd2.cache()\n\nrdd2.count()  # Materializes the cache\nrdd2.sum()    # Can reuse cached partitions\n```\n\nSpark also supports different storage levels through `persist()`.\n\nImportant: RDDs are not automatically cached. They are recomputed when needed unless persisted or otherwise materialized.\n\n### 6. Parallel Processing\n\nRDDs support parallel execution across multiple CPU cores and machines.\n\nFor example:\n\n- An RDD contains 100 partitions.\n- The cluster has 5 Executors.\n- Each Executor has 4 CPU cores.\n- Each task requires 1 CPU core.\n\nSpark can execute up to 20 tasks concurrently, assuming all resources are available.\n\nThe remaining tasks are scheduled as execution slots become free.\n\n### 7. Coarse-Grained Transformations\n\nRDDs primarily support coarse-grained operations, where transformations are applied to collections or partitions rather than individual mutable records.\n\nExamples:\n\n- `map()`\n- `filter()`\n- `flatMap()`\n- `reduceByKey()`\n\nRDDs are not designed for random, in-place updates to individual elements like a traditional database.\n\n## 2. Five Internal Properties of an RDD\n\nFor deeper technical interviews, Spark's RDD abstraction is defined by five important pieces of information.\n\n| Internal property              | Explanation                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Partitions                     | The list of partitions into which the RDD is divided                                     |\n| Compute function               | A function responsible for computing each partition                                      |\n| Dependencies                   | Information about parent RDDs required to compute the current RDD                        |\n| Partitioner (optional)         | Defines how key-value data is partitioned, when applicable                               |\n| Preferred locations (optional) | Information about preferred locations for computing partitions, supporting data locality |\n\nThese properties help Spark determine what data to process, how to compute it, what dependencies exist, and where computation can run efficiently.\n\n## 3. Interview-ready answer (60 seconds)\n\n> The key properties of an RDD are immutability, distribution, partitioning, fault tolerance, lazy evaluation, and support for in-memory persistence.\n>\n> First, RDDs are immutable, meaning every transformation creates a new RDD instead of modifying an existing one.\n>\n> Second, RDDs are distributed into multiple partitions, which allows Spark to process data in parallel across Executors.\n>\n> Third, RDDs are fault-tolerant because Spark maintains lineage information, allowing lost partitions to be recomputed after failures.\n>\n> Fourth, RDDs follow lazy evaluation, where transformations are not executed until an action is triggered.\n>\n> Fifth, RDDs support caching and persistence, making repeated computations more efficient.\n>\n> Internally, an RDD is characterized by its partitions, compute function, dependencies, optional partitioner, and optional preferred locations. These properties allow Spark to schedule distributed computations and recover from failures.\n\nKey takeaway: An RDD is Immutable + Distributed + Partitioned + Fault-Tolerant + Lazy, with optional caching and persistence for efficient reuse."
